<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Strength Through Joy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kraft durch Freude</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Strength Through Joy was the leisure organisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-labour-front">German Labour Front</a>, founded in November 1933. It offered workers cheap holidays, cruises, concerts, sport and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, aiming to win support for the regime and raise productivity, and it began the giant seaside resort of Prora on Rügen. The Volkswagen car was first promoted as the KdF-Wagen, after the organisation's German initials.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/strength-through-joy.jpg" alt="Strength Through Joy" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ericmetro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KdF_Symbol.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Strength Through Joy, in German Kraft durch Freude or KdF, was announced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ley">Robert Ley</a> on 27 November 1933 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-labour-front">German Labour Front</a>. Its aim was to give Germans access to leisure once reserved for the privileged, such as cruises and car ownership. The government feared that rising wages would slow rearmament, and so chose to raise living standards in other ways and win support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a>, paying for the programme with deductions from workers' wages. It hoped to bridge class divisions and build a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volksgemeinschaft">Volksgemeinschaft</a>, or people's community, with a population fit for work and military service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>KdF had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> for sports, adult education, leisure time, folklore and homeland, and travel, hiking and holidays. The last, headed by Bodo Lafferentz, was the largest and most profitable. A further department, Beauty of Labour, improved workplaces through toilets, washing rooms, better air and less noise, and by 1938 had improved the conditions of 34,000 workplaces, largely through unpaid overtime. The Leisure Time Office offered discounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and concert tickets, with film buses and mobile stages in rural areas.</p><p>Official statistics gave 2.3 million KdF holidaymakers in 1934 and 10.3 million in 1938, and by 1939 it was the world's largest tourism operator. Cruises were its best-known offering. Six large ships, among them the Wilhelm Gustloff, were built for KdF and six more chartered, with trips to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Most cruise passengers came from the middle class rather than the working class. The government planted spies on the ships to watch the passengers' behaviour. Some KdF travellers complained of poor conditions and regimented schedules, while many on domestic trips were enthusiastic, especially those who had never travelled before.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the outbreak of war in 1939 most programmes were suspended, and projects such as the Prora holiday resort were never completed. By then KdF had sold more than 45 million package tours and excursions, including over 700,000 sea voyages. It turned to troop entertainment, staging over 300,000 performances by the end of 1941, including for SS guards in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>. Its car, the KdF-Wagen, was meant to sell for 1,000 Reichsmarks or less through a stamp-savings scheme. No private buyer received one before the war ended, and the factory at Fallersleben turned to military vehicles. The car later became the Volkswagen Beetle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Strength Through Joy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
